sections[6]:
  - id: scope-decisions
    title: Scope and decisions
    kind: rules
    items[6]{title,text}:
      Primary authoring model,"Top-level namespaced fn test_*(t: goosetest::Test&, optional_fixture) declarations; optional fresh Fixture and named hooks. No per-test manual registration."
      Directory contract,"src/ and test/ are a strict bidirectional unit mirror. integration/ has independent *_test.goose suites. test_support/ holds helper modules. Intentionally invalid fixture source uses a data suffix, not .goose, until materialized in a disposable project."
      One-time consumer root,"goosetest_root.goose holds ordinary Goose package declarations, imports generated orchestration, and returns its result. The generator never reconstructs a production root or rewrites package sources."
      Execution and failure,Sequential native compilation/execution via goose build --debug. Framework assertions recover at explicit boundaries; fatal runtime errors fail the whole run. Compile/run deadlines bound hung stages.
      First-release metadata,"Only @DisplayName and @Disabled are supported. Unknown framework annotations are errors, including POC @Tag and @RepeatedTest until those features are deliberately added."
      Explicit exclusions,"No benchmarks, metrics/telemetry, feature-flag infrastructure, shadow or A/B rollout, or smoke checks. Also defer parallel execution, expected-abort/contained workers, property testing, recursive deep diffs, parameterized/repeated tests, tags, snapshots, watch mode, coverage instrumentation, JUnit XML/TAP/JSON export, reporter plugins, and Windows support."
  - id: evidence
    title: Verified starting points and corrections to the POC
    kind: notes
    items[6]{title,text}:
      Execution evidence,"After correcting the generator’s obsolete absolute docs/example_project path in a temporary copy, the generated suite ran with 3 passed, 1 failed, and 1 skipped, yet the shell status was 0. The inline and contained demos also calculate failure status without returning it."
      Style evidence,"goosefmt check poc failed. The POC uses compressed statements, broad global declarations, and functions exceeding the guide’s intended limits. Shipping code and generated output need the actual style contract, not cosmetic cleanup alone."
      Silent omissions,The scanner suppresses directory/file read errors and drops paths/cases at fixed caps; result spans can also be dropped. These are framework correctness defects because they can make incomplete test runs look successful.
      Compiler version matters,A local compile probe rejects namespaced receiver UFCS that the current online reference supports. The installed local docs confirm the old lookup behavior. Use the verified baseline and small global bridges rather than assuming online syntax works locally.
      Process API gap,"The installed stdlib/os.goose has file, environment, stream, clock, and directory operations but no subprocess API. A small audited native process boundary is necessary for a real one-command runner on this baseline."
      Containment limits,"The contained-worker POC builds and catches expected aborts, but stack overflow, exit, and some native failures still terminate the program; foreign resources are not guaranteed to close. It is not a general sandbox or first-release isolation strategy."
  - id: architecture
    title: Architecture and durable contracts
    kind: architecture
    items[6]{title,text}:
      Runtime package,"goosetest.goose is a facade over src/runtime/*.goose. Namespaced state and implementations own comparison, recovery boundaries, and results. Deliberate global bridges expose ergonomic assertion UFCS on the pinned compiler."
      Discovery,Bounded filesystem walk → lexical scan → validated manifest → bidirectional mirror audit. Stable IDs and source locations are fixed here; selection never changes the audited inventory.
      Generation and execution,Validated manifest → deterministic modules under goosetest_generated/ → existing goosetest_root.goose → compiler child → test child. Direct calls preserve static typing and long-distance return ancestry.
      Result ownership,"Fixed records plus owned diagnostic bytes, with borrowed contexts when multiple growable tables coexist. No references into dead fixtures; no unbounded log accumulation; no silent record drops."
      Reporting boundary,Structured outcomes feed a plain console renderer. The runtime need not print and has no knowledge of CLI flags. Export formats remain future consumers of the same model.
      Platform boundary,"CLI-only native process/filesystem declarations live in one canonical module per interface. The package runtime does not link those adapters. Direct argv invocation, explicit cwd, deadlines, child reaping, and artifact ownership are the boundary’s responsibilities."
  - id: exit-policy
    title: Observable outcome policy
    kind: rules
    items[5]{title,text}:
      Status 0,A nonempty selection completed with no case/hook failures. An explicitly disabled nonempty selection is valid and reports skips.
      Status 1,One or more framework assertions or recoverable lifecycle hooks failed. Remaining cases execute unless --fail-fast was requested.
      Status 2,"Usage, layout, discovery, compilation, generation, child-process, deadline, fatal runtime, or resource-limit error; also no discovered or selected cases. The output must say when the run is incomplete."
      Interruption,"Forward cancellation to the owned child stage, reap it, and preserve a conventional nonzero interruption result. Cancellation never counts as a passing or merely skipped suite."
      Cleanup contract,"After framework setup/body failure, attempt the corresponding cleanup through its own recoverable boundary. Preserve both causes when cleanup fails. No cleanup guarantee after runtime abort, exit, process death, or deadline termination."
  - id: performance
    title: Performance and memory rules
    kind: rules
    items[6]{title,text}:
      "Linear scanning, ordered matching","Scan each source once, maintain cursor progress, sort path inventory once, and use a linear merge or bounded lookup for mirror matching. Avoid the POC’s nested source-by-test search."
      Cold diagnostics,"Passing checks perform comparisons and counters only. Format values on failure into bounded storage, avoiding repeated copies and intermediate strings."
      Static execution,"Compile the discovered program once per invocation with direct case calls. Do not add function-pointer registries, plugin dispatch, caching invalidation, or worker scheduling before there is a demonstrated need."
      Named finite budgets,"Bound filesystem entries, path/file/total bytes, nesting, cases, failure records, diagnostic bytes, stage duration, and termination grace. Exercise limits with small injected values; pick documented production defaults after real fixtures fit."
      Style is a design constraint,The 500-line file and 70-line function limits apply to generated code too. Split generated wrappers/dispatch accordingly rather than bypassing the rules with a huge main function.
      Validation without performance theater,"Use structural complexity review and exact completeness tests across old capacity thresholds. Do not add benchmark suites, timing gates, dashboards, or telemetry."
  - id: parallelism
    title: Independent delivery areas
    kind: ordered-steps
    items[4]{title,text}:
      Freeze small interfaces first,"E1 fixes Test/CaseResult/Failure ownership, and E2 fixes Manifest/CaseId/SourceLocation. Keep these records small and documented; do not design a generalized plugin protocol."
      Independent tracks,"After E1.S1, discovery E2 and process adapter E3.S2 can proceed independently of runtime work. After E1.S4, rich diagnostics E5 can proceed using the explicit harness without waiting for generated execution."
      Integration points,E3.S1 joins the runtime with discovery. E3.S3 adds the process adapter. E4 builds lifecycle behavior on that working runner. E6 joins finished behavior and external verification.
      Ownership boundaries,"Assign discovery, native process execution, and runtime/reporting modules separately if implementation is later delegated. Do not have parallel workers independently redefine the common result or manifest records."
epics[6]:
  - id: E1
    title: An importable runtime that passes and fails correctly
    summary: "Deliver a small, executable test package before discovery exists. A separate example imports the actual package, runs multiple cases, and returns a reliable process status."
    description: "<a id=\"framework-design-reference\"></a>\n\n### Framework design reference: what we are building\n\nThe examples below define the **intended first-release developer experience**. They are design targets for the stories, not claims that goosetest already implements these APIs. The linked POC files are existing reference implementations. Where the target differs, the change is called out explicitly.\n\nThe main influence is the repository’s **JUnit-style discovered-function POC**: ordinary named test functions, a fresh fixture per case, lifecycle hooks, hard/soft assertions, and useful expected/actual diagnostics. The inline describe DSL and contained-worker prototype remain reference material for deferred alternatives.\n\n- **Authoring:** [Auth test authoring example — line 12, `struct Fixture`](/Users/bob/repos/goosetest/poc/example_project/test/auth/token_test.goose) and [Permissions test and struct-diff example — line 30, `fn test_viewer_write_denied_demo_diff`](/Users/bob/repos/goosetest/poc/example_project/test/workspaces/permissions_test.goose).\n- **Runtime:** [Runtime recovery boundary — line 56, `fn run_case`](/Users/bob/repos/goosetest/poc/example_project/goosetest.goose), [Expected-first assertion implementation — line 161, `fn assert_eq`](/Users/bob/repos/goosetest/poc/example_project/goosetest.goose), and [Soft assertion implementation — line 194, `fn check_eq`](/Users/bob/repos/goosetest/poc/example_project/goosetest.goose).\n- **Discovery/generation:** [POC discovery and lifecycle emission — line 126, `fn scan_and_emit_suite`](/Users/bob/repos/goosetest/poc/junit_runner_poc.goose) and [POC mirror audit and generation — line 266, `fn generate_junit_runner`](/Users/bob/repos/goosetest/poc/junit_runner_poc.goose).\n- **Formatting and code shape:** [Normative Goose style guide](/Users/bob/repos/goosefmt/docs/styleguide.md) is normative whenever the prototype conflicts with it.\n\n**Reference tour:** [unit-test API](#reference-unit-api) · [consumer layout](#reference-project-layout) · [test root and generation](#reference-test-root) · [fixture lifecycle](#reference-lifecycle) · [integration test](#reference-integration-api) · [assertion signatures](#reference-assertion-api) · [console output](#reference-console) · [source-to-story map](#reference-source-map).\n\n<a id=\"reference-unit-api\"></a>\n\n### Target unit-test file: test/auth/token_test.goose\n\n```goose\n// Verifies valid, malformed, and disabled token cases with a fresh fixture.\nnamespace auth_token_test;\n\nimport goosetest;\n\nimport src.auth.token;\n\nstruct Fixture {\n    header: const u8[:] = \"\",\n}\n\nfn before_each(f: Fixture&) {\n    f.header = \"Bearer tok_admin\";\n}\n\nfn after_each(f: Fixture&) {\n    f.header = \"\";\n}\n\n// @DisplayName(\"parses valid admin Bearer token into claims\")\nfn test_valid_admin_bearer(t: goosetest::Test&, f: Fixture&) {\n    let claims = auth_token::parse_bearer(f.header);\n    t.assert_true(claims.valid, \"admin token must be valid\");\n    t.assert_eq(1, claims.user_id);\n    t.assert_eq(\"admin\", claims.scope);\n}\n\n// @DisplayName(\"rejects malformed Authorization header\")\nfn test_malformed_header(t: goosetest::Test&) {\n    let claims = auth_token::parse_bearer(\"Basic abcdef\");\n    t.check_eq(false, claims.valid, \"Basic authentication is unsupported\");\n    t.assert_eq(0, claims.user_id);\n}\n\n// @Disabled(\"pending clock skew tolerance in token verification\")\nfn test_expired_token_clock_skew(t: goosetest::Test&) {\n    t.fail(\"not implemented\");\n}\n```\n\nThis is a complete target test file. It has no main function, registration list, test class, assertion macro, or manual runner construction. Tests explicitly import the modules they use. The first test receives a fresh fixture; the other two are fixture-free even though the file declares Fixture. The fixture hooks apply to the fixture-using case.\n\nThe hard `assert_*` family stops its current test or hook phase; `check_eq` records a failure and continues. The disabled case remains typechecked while its body and hooks remain unexecuted. `@DisplayName` affects display only; the function name remains part of the stable ID.\n\nChanges from the POC: the context is `goosetest::Test`; declarations follow the guide’s header/namespace/import order; `@Tag` and `@RepeatedTest` are deferred; diagnostic and exit-status defects are corrected. This file is delivered progressively: E1 establishes assertions, E3 establishes generated execution, and E4 completes hooks and metadata.\n\nExisting authoring reference: [Auth test authoring example — line 12, `struct Fixture`](/Users/bob/repos/goosetest/poc/example_project/test/auth/token_test.goose). Production behavior used here: [Auth production module — line 11, `fn parse_bearer`](/Users/bob/repos/goosetest/poc/example_project/src/auth/token.goose).\n\n**Normative review rule:** an implementer should be able to point to a target example and its source reference before changing the public API. If compiler evidence requires a different spelling, update this reference and the affected story criteria together rather than silently diverging.\n\n\nDelivery: a real package with observable pass/fail behavior. Later epics consume this runtime; they do not replace it. The short explicit harness is a package-API example and a bootstrap oracle, not the eventual user registration workflow."
    dependencies: []
    stories[4]:
      - id: E1.S1
        title: Pin the compiler contract and establish enforceable style
        details[5]: "Use the locally verified Goose v2026.3 release as the initial baseline. Record its installation identity and supported commands; do not use goose --version, which this release rejects. Do not upgrade the compiler as part of this plan.","Add small compile fixtures for package-relative imports, const-generic inputs, generic long-distance returns, global bridge calls, and references into growable storage. Check the exact syntax before exposing an API.","Adopt the settings in /Users/bob/repos/goosefmt/.goosefmt.toml and the normative rules in docs/styleguide.md from that repository. Use headers, namespace-before-import order, direct imports, snake_case names, 120 columns, at most 500 lines per file, and at most 70 lines per function.","Keep the public type namespaced as goosetest::Test. Use namespaced implementations and only the deliberate global assertion bridges needed for t.assert_eq(...) on this compiler. Keep execution helpers qualified and document possible same-name shadowing. All proposed source paths are initial module boundaries; split further only when the guide or a cohesive responsibility requires it.",Record the baseline platform as the available macOS arm64 environment. Keep the process adapter separate so another platform can be added later without changing the runtime API.
        acceptanceCriteria[4]: The compatibility document names the supported release and explains why current online UFCS/defer behavior must not be assumed.,Compiler fixtures demonstrate a namespaced test calling the chosen assertion bridge and a hard assertion returning to the intended execution boundary.,The style configuration is explicit; new framework files pass the installed goosefmt check. Compiler-sensitive formatter defects are recorded with a reproducer rather than hidden by broad rule exclusions.,No source file depends on a sibling declaration solely through an accidental transitive import.
        unitTests[2]: Compile positive fixtures and assert failure for an assertion called outside its required execution boundary.,Check that a bridge works inside two different user namespaces and that the documented shadowing case produces the expected compiler diagnostic.
        filesLikelyTouched[4]: .goosefmt.toml,docs/compatibility.md,integration/compatibility_test.goose,integration/fixtures/compatibility/README.md
        dependencies: []
        risk: high
        description: "**Design target:** [Concrete example in this plan](#reference-unit-api).\n\n**Existing source references:**\n\n- [Normative Goose style guide](/Users/bob/repos/goosefmt/docs/styleguide.md)\n- [Style guide: namespaces and bridges — line 186, `### 3.2`](/Users/bob/repos/goosefmt/docs/styleguide.md)\n- [Pinned compiler package/import contract — line 92, `**Only the root file declares packages.**`](/Users/bob/.local/share/goose/v2026.3/goose-v2026.3-macos-arm64/docs/packages.md)\n\n**Retain or change:** Preserve the target test-call spelling while verifying the pinned compiler and applying the guide."
      - id: E1.S2
        title: Implement one-case execution and fundamental assertions
        details[5]: "Create goosetest::Test and a qualified run_case boundary. The context represents the current case, not the entire CLI, manifest, and reporter combined.","Implement fail, assert_true, assert_false, assert_eq, assert_ne, and check_eq. Equality consistently takes expected before actual; check_eq records a failure and continues, while hard assertions stop the current phase.",Use the non-local return mechanism verified in E1.S1. Do not use built-in assert for ordinary test failures; reserve it for framework invariants.,Reset per-case state before every execution and calculate the final case outcome from all of its failures. A later successful check must never erase an earlier failure.,"Keep successful assertion paths free of message formatting. Format diagnostic values while their inputs are alive, using Goose format hooks."
        acceptanceCriteria[4]: A hard failure prevents the next statement in that case and permits the following case to execute.,"Two failed soft checks both appear in the result, and the case is counted as failed once.","Scalar, string, and slice equality compile through the public API; expected and actual labels are never reversed.",A hand-written harness returns 0 for passing cases and 1 when any case fails.
        unitTests[2]: "Use state sentinels to verify hard-stop, soft-continue, and next-case execution.","Exercise zero assertions, successful comparisons, and multiple failures without relying on console string parsing."
        filesLikelyTouched[7]: goosetest.goose,src/runtime/context.goose,src/runtime/execution.goose,src/runtime/assertions.goose,test/runtime/context_test.goose,test/runtime/execution_test.goose,test/runtime/assertions_test.goose
        dependencies[1]: E1.S1
        risk: high
        description: "**Design target:** [Concrete example in this plan](#reference-assertion-api).\n\n**Existing source references:**\n\n- [Runtime recovery boundary — line 56, `fn run_case`](/Users/bob/repos/goosetest/poc/example_project/goosetest.goose)\n- [Expected-first assertion implementation — line 161, `fn assert_eq`](/Users/bob/repos/goosetest/poc/example_project/goosetest.goose)\n- [Soft assertion implementation — line 194, `fn check_eq`](/Users/bob/repos/goosetest/poc/example_project/goosetest.goose)\n\n**Retain or change:** Carry forward hard-stop/soft-continue behavior and expected-first arguments; separate runtime state from printing."
      - id: E1.S3
        title: Own structured results and diagnostics with explicit bounds
        details[6]: "Define small named records for case identity, case outcome, phase, failure spans, and totals. Represent pass, fail, skip, and runner error explicitly; keep filtered-out cases separate from skipped cases.",Keep stable metadata and diagnostic bytes in owner scopes that outlive reporting. Use offsets into bounded byte arenas for stored messages; never retain slices of a temporary formatted value.,"Respect Goose layout constraints: one resizable tail per owning struct. If results and bytes both grow, own them in driver locals and pass a borrowed context of references.","Introduce a named Limits record covering case records, failures, and diagnostic bytes. Check before appending; use a fixed, allocation-independent terminal diagnostic when storage is exhausted.","A limit breach is a runner error with status 2. Preserve already recorded failures, mark the run incomplete, and never return a successful result after dropping a record. These are framework-owned storage bounds; they do not sandbox allocations or work performed by tests or custom format hooks.",Keep result collection independent from terminal rendering. Do not introduce a reporter plugin interface or a serialized interchange format yet.
        acceptanceCriteria[4]: A result remains valid after the test fixture and assertion arguments leave scope.,At-limit workloads complete; the next record or byte triggers a visible incomplete-run error without a panic or silent omission.,Totals reconcile with emitted case outcomes and do not count one failed case multiple times.,The runtime can run with no console renderer attached.
        unitTests[2]: "Exercise tiny injected limits for case count, failure count, and message bytes.",Record messages from nested helper scopes and inspect them only after those helpers return.
        filesLikelyTouched[6]: src/runtime/results.goose,src/runtime/diagnostics.goose,src/runtime/context.goose,test/runtime/context_test.goose,test/runtime/results_test.goose,test/runtime/diagnostics_test.goose
        dependencies[1]: E1.S2
        risk: high
        description: "**Design target:** [Concrete example in this plan](#reference-console).\n\n**Existing source references:**\n\n- [POC state and failure storage — line 19, `struct Test`](/Users/bob/repos/goosetest/poc/example_project/goosetest.goose)\n- [Style guide: multiple growable collections — line 425, `### 4.6`](/Users/bob/repos/goosefmt/docs/styleguide.md)\n- [Style guide: bounded control flow and storage — line 843, `### 7.8`](/Users/bob/repos/goosefmt/docs/styleguide.md)\n\n**Retain or change:** Replace the fixed failure-span cap with bounded, owned results that cannot silently disappear."
      - id: E1.S4
        title: Prove package consumption with a standalone example
        details[4]: Make root goosetest.goose a small package facade importing runtime modules. Keep CLI-only dependencies and native process code out of the importable package.,Create a small example project with mirrored src and test files plus a temporary hand-written harness using the real package import. Do not copy the POC runtime into the example.,"Run a passing variant and a deliberately failing variant through an independent process-level checker. Preserve intentional negative cases as test data, not as default failing product examples.",Document the public assertion order and the difference between framework assertions and fatal Goose runtime checks.
        acceptanceCriteria[5]: An application outside the package directory imports goosetest and compiles without relying on the framework repository as its import root.,The passing program exits 0; the deliberately failing program exits 1 and still executes a later case.,The runtime needs only Goose standard-library modules and the compiler runtime.,Example source passes the same style checks as framework source.,"The embedded unit-test API example is the authoring reference; after lifecycle stories land, it compiles against the real package without manual test registration."
        unitTests[2]: Build and execute the example from its root and from another working directory using explicit paths.,Verify OS exit status independently of the framework summary text.
        filesLikelyTouched[4]: examples/basic/goosetest_root.goose,examples/basic/src/calculator.goose,examples/basic/test/calculator_test.goose,integration/package_test.goose
        dependencies[1]: E1.S3
        risk: medium
        description: "**Design target:** [Concrete example in this plan](#reference-unit-api).\n\n**Existing source references:**\n\n- [Auth test authoring example — line 12, `struct Fixture`](/Users/bob/repos/goosetest/poc/example_project/test/auth/token_test.goose)\n- [Permissions test and struct-diff example — line 30, `fn test_viewer_write_denied_demo_diff`](/Users/bob/repos/goosetest/poc/example_project/test/workspaces/permissions_test.goose)\n- [Pinned compiler package/import contract — line 92, `**Only the root file declares packages.**`](/Users/bob/.local/share/goose/v2026.3/goose-v2026.3-macos-arm64/docs/packages.md)\n\n**Retain or change:** Make the embedded target files consume the real package; keep default examples passing."
    shortTitle: Runtime foundation
  - id: E2
    title: Discover and audit a project without compiling it
    summary: Deliver goosetest list and goosetest check-mirror. Users can inspect every discovered case and diagnose layout problems before execution is integrated.
    description: "<a id=\"reference-project-layout\"></a>\n\n### Target consumer project layout\n\n```text\nworkspace/\n├── goosetest/                         # Framework checkout or installed package.\n└── my_app/\n    ├── main.goose                     # Production entry, if the app needs one.\n    ├── goosetest_root.goose           # Authored once; declares test packages.\n    ├── src/\n    │   ├── auth/token.goose\n    │   └── workspaces/permissions.goose\n    ├── test/                         # Exact unit mirror of src/.\n    │   ├── auth/token_test.goose\n    │   └── workspaces/permissions_test.goose\n    ├── integration/\n    │   └── access_flow_test.goose     # Cross-module tests; no source mirror.\n    ├── test_support/                 # Optional helper modules; not discovered.\n    └── goosetest_generated/           # Generated, ignored, never hand edited.\n        ├── runner.goose\n        ├── suite_0000.goose\n        └── ...                       # Additional bounded suite/dispatch files.\n```\n\nThe two production modules each have exactly one unit-test counterpart. The integration case may exercise both without requiring a fictitious source module. A root entry, the test root, test helpers, and generated files are outside the unit mirror. Nested integration fixture data must not masquerade as discovered `*_test.goose` files.\n\nSource modules for this reference scenario are the existing POC auth and permissions implementations. The first-release examples should format those implementations to the guide and use the real goosetest package rather than copy the POC runtime.\n\nSource references: [POC mirror audit and generation — line 266, `fn generate_junit_runner`](/Users/bob/repos/goosetest/poc/junit_runner_poc.goose); [Auth production module — line 11, `fn parse_bearer`](/Users/bob/repos/goosetest/poc/example_project/src/auth/token.goose); [Permissions production module — line 12, `fn check_write_access`](/Users/bob/repos/goosetest/poc/example_project/src/workspaces/permissions.goose).\n\n<a id=\"reference-list-output\"></a>\n\n### Target discovery output and stable IDs\n\nThe case ID format is `<relative test path>::<namespace>::<function>`. Paths use forward slashes; display names never alter the ID. Here is an excerpt for the auth unit suite:\n\n```text\n$ goosetest list --project ./my_app --filter auth_token_test\nunit  test/auth/token_test.goose::auth_token_test::test_valid_admin_bearer\nunit  test/auth/token_test.goose::auth_token_test::test_malformed_header\nunit  test/auth/token_test.goose::auth_token_test::test_expired_token_clock_skew\n      disabled: pending clock skew tolerance in token verification\n3 selected cases: 2 executable, 1 disabled\n```\n\n`--filter` becomes available in E4.S4; E2.S4 initially lists all cases. Listing also includes the source declaration line; spacing and labels above are illustrative, while stable IDs and selection counts are contractual.\n\n```text\n$ goosetest check-mirror --project ./my_app\nMIRROR OK  src/auth/token.goose -> test/auth/token_test.goose\nMIRROR OK  src/workspaces/permissions.goose -> test/workspaces/permissions_test.goose\nUnit mirrors: 2 matched, 0 missing, 0 orphaned\n\n# If the permissions unit file is removed:\nMISSING TEST  src/workspaces/permissions.goose\n              expected test/workspaces/permissions_test.goose\n```\n\nThe valid audit returns 0; the missing mirror returns 2 before compilation. These are file-correspondence results, not code-coverage claims.\n\nDelivery: useful read-only commands. E2 can be developed independently of assertion implementation once shared path/diagnostic conventions are agreed; it has no dependency on native process execution."
    dependencies: []
    stories[4]:
      - id: E2.S1
        title: Walk source and test roots completely and deterministically
        details[6]: "Walk src/, test/, and optional integration/ iteratively. Treat test_support/ as non-discovered helpers and goosetest_generated/ as framework-owned output outside the audited roots.",Validate path segments used in Goose dotted imports. Reject names the compiler cannot import rather than generating invalid code; permit the project root itself to contain spaces.,"Bound visited entries, nesting, path bytes, accumulated path bytes, per-file source bytes, and total scanned bytes through named limits. Report the offending path and limit.",Treat directory-list and file-read failures as errors. Missing src/ or test/ is an invalid unit-test project; an absent integration/ is valid.,"Reject symlink traversal under the scanned roots. The pinned standard library lacks lstat-style inspection, so add a narrow filesystem adapter with canonical extern declarations and main.build wiring here; E3 later extends it for artifact ownership. Inspect entry kinds without following links so cycles cannot be traversed. This adapter adds no third-party package.",Normalize relative separators and sort paths bytewise once. Do not use nested source-by-test linear searches.
        acceptanceCriteria[4]: Discovery returns the same path sequence regardless of directory creation order.,More than the POC limits of 128 files or 64 directories can be handled within the configured bounds; no entry is silently omitted.,"Unreadable entries, invalid import segments, limit breaches, and cycles cannot produce a successful incomplete manifest.",The walker owns path storage for as long as any manifest record references it.
        unitTests[2]: "Use temporary directory fixtures covering empty trees, nested files, spaces in the project path, unreadable entries, and a cycle.",Inject small limits and verify exact-bound success and one-over-bound failure.
