        dependencies[1]: E4.S2
        risk: high
        description: "**Design target:** [Concrete example in this plan](#reference-unit-api).\n\n**Existing source references:**\n\n- [Disabled call-graph preservation — line 224, `Keep disabled test in`](/Users/bob/repos/goosetest/poc/junit_runner_poc.goose)\n- [Auth test authoring example — line 12, `struct Fixture`](/Users/bob/repos/goosetest/poc/example_project/test/auth/token_test.goose)\n\n**Retain or change:** Preserve disabled-case compilation and the explicit reason without executing the fixture."
      - id: E4.S4
        title: Select stable case IDs and stop early without skipping cleanup
        details[5]: Add --filter TEXT as a literal substring match against stable case IDs and --suite unit|integration|all. Reuse the same selector for list and test; keep the default as all.,Audit and compile all discovered tests. A filter controls execution only; unselected tests stay in valid unreachable call paths so their assertions still typecheck.,"Add --fail-fast as an execution policy. After the current case fails, finish its cleanup and suite cleanup before stopping further case execution.","Keep excluded, disabled, setup-blocked, and fail-fast-not-run cases distinguishable. Do not inflate the skipped count with every case excluded by a filter.",Return status 2 for an explicit filter with zero matches and for a project with zero cases. Preserve status 1 when fail-fast stops after an ordinary failure.
        acceptanceCriteria[4]: "The same filter selects identical IDs in list and test, independent of display-name changes.",An unselected malformed test still fails compilation and cannot silently rot.,Fail-fast runs current-case and suite cleanup exactly once and executes no subsequent body.,Selection output makes zero matches distinguishable from a nonempty disabled selection.
        unitTests[2]: "Test unit/integration selection, literal special characters in filters, and zero-match status.",Verify call-graph validity for filtered assertions and cleanup order under fail-fast.
