        dependencies[1]: E4.S3
        risk: medium
        description: "**Design target:** [Concrete example in this plan](#reference-list-output).\n\n**Existing source references:**\n\n- [POC literal filter behavior — line 102, `fn matches_filter`](/Users/bob/repos/goosetest/poc/goosetest_poc.goose)\n- [Disabled call-graph preservation — line 224, `Keep disabled test in`](/Users/bob/repos/goosetest/poc/junit_runner_poc.goose)\n- [POC discovery and lifecycle emission — line 126, `fn scan_and_emit_suite`](/Users/bob/repos/goosetest/poc/junit_runner_poc.goose)\n\n**Retain or change:** Apply selection to stable IDs and keep filtered tests valid in the whole-program call graph."
    shortTitle: Fixtures & lifecycle
  - id: E5
    title: Actionable assertions and readable failure reports
    summary: Deliver useful expected/actual diagnostics and terminal output from structured results. This work can proceed against E1’s explicit harness while discovery and execution are built.
    description: "<a id=\"reference-assertion-api\"></a>\n\n### Target assertion API at the call site\n\nThese are call-site signatures, not a second implementation. `message` is optional except for `fail`; the default is an empty user label while generated expected/actual details remain present.\n\n| Call | Behavior |\n| --- | --- |\n| `t.assert_true(condition, message)` | Require true; stop this phase on failure. |\n| `t.assert_false(condition, message)` | Require false; stop this phase on failure. |\n| `t.assert_eq(expected, actual, message)` | Compare scalar/string/slice values; expected comes first. |\n| `t.assert_ne(unexpected, actual, message)` | Require a different actual value. |\n| `t.check_eq(expected, actual, message)` | Record a failed comparison and continue. |\n| `t.assert_struct_eq(expected, actual, message)` | Show all mismatching top-level fields, then stop this phase. |\n| `t.assert_contains(expected_fragment, actual_text, message)` | Require the fragment to occur in the actual text. |\n| `t.assert_near(expected, actual, absolute_tolerance, message)` | Compare with the documented finite tolerance/NaN policy. |\n| `t.fail(message)` | Record an explicit failure and stop this phase. |\n\nThe global functions supporting `t.assert_*` are deliberately narrow bridges over namespaced implementations on the pinned compiler. Test authors do not need to understand that implementation detail. The API consistently uses the JUnit POC’s expected-first convention; the alternate inline POC uses actual-first in places and must not be copied unchanged.\n\nImplementation references: [Expected-first assertion implementation — line 161, `fn assert_eq`](/Users/bob/repos/goosetest/poc/example_project/goosetest.goose); [Soft assertion implementation — line 194, `fn check_eq`](/Users/bob/repos/goosetest/poc/example_project/goosetest.goose); [Top-level struct-field diff — line 209, `fn assert_struct_eq`](/Users/bob/repos/goosetest/poc/example_project/goosetest.goose); [POC numeric comparison — line 214, `fn expect_near`](/Users/bob/repos/goosetest/poc/goosetest_poc.goose); [POC string containment — line 225, `fn expect_contains`](/Users/bob/repos/goosetest/poc/goosetest_poc.goose).\n\n<a id=\"reference-permission-test\"></a>\n\n### Target struct assertion: test/workspaces/permissions_test.goose\n\n```goose\n// Verifies permission decisions and shows field-level failure diagnostics.\nnamespace workspaces_permissions_test;\n\nimport goosetest;\n\nimport src.workspaces.permissions;\n\n// @DisplayName(\"grants write access to admin token\")\nfn test_admin_can_write(t: goosetest::Test&) {\n    let actual = workspaces_permissions::check_write_access(\"Bearer tok_admin\");\n    t.assert_true(actual.allowed);\n    t.assert_eq(\"admin\", actual.effective_role);\n}\n\n// @DisplayName(\"denies write access to viewer role\")\nfn test_viewer_cannot_write(t: goosetest::Test&) {\n    let actual = workspaces_permissions::check_write_access(\"Bearer tok_viewer\");\n    let expected = workspaces_permissions::AccessDecision {\n        allowed: false,\n        effective_role: \"viewer\",\n        reason: \"insufficient_role\",\n    };\n    t.assert_struct_eq(expected, actual, \"viewer write check\");\n}\n```\n\nThe shipped example above passes. To validate the failure renderer, a disposable negative fixture changes the viewer expectation to `allowed: true` and `reason: \"ok\"`, matching the intentional mismatch in the POC.\n\n<a id=\"reference-console\"></a>\n\n### Target console output: the same negative fixture\n\n```text\n$ goosetest test --project ./my_app\nauth_token_test\n  PASS  parses valid admin Bearer token into claims\n  PASS  rejects malformed Authorization header\n  SKIP  test_expired_token_clock_skew\n        pending clock skew tolerance in token verification\n\nworkspaces_permissions_test\n  PASS  grants write access to admin token\n  FAIL  denies write access to viewer role\n\naccess_flow_test\n  PASS  test_viewer_token_cannot_write\n\nFAIL test/workspaces/permissions_test.goose::workspaces_permissions_test::test_viewer_cannot_write\n  Test declaration: test/workspaces/permissions_test.goose:16\n  Phase: test\n  viewer write check\n    .allowed  expected: <true>  actual: <false>\n    .reason   expected: <ok>    actual: <insufficient_role>\n\nTests: 4 passed, 1 failed, 1 skipped\nSuite hook failures: 0\nExit status: 1\n```\n\nThe three suite groups are a reference layout; the final implementation uses the deterministic order defined by discovery. The six case outcomes and status are contractual. `Exit status: 1` shows the actual process result, not merely a printed number. Without the intentional negative mutation, the same project reports 5 passed, 0 failed, 1 skipped and returns 0.\n\nThe line shown is explicitly the test declaration location. Do not label it as the exact failing assertion line without compiler support. ASCII PASS/FAIL/SKIP is the portable baseline; terminal colors and box-drawing are unnecessary.\n\nOutput reference: [POC console summary — line 235, `fn summary`](/Users/bob/repos/goosetest/poc/example_project/goosetest.goose). Original intentional field mismatch: [Permissions test and struct-diff example — line 30, `fn test_viewer_write_denied_demo_diff`](/Users/bob/repos/goosetest/poc/example_project/test/workspaces/permissions_test.goose).\n\nDelivery: better diagnostics in an already runnable package. E5 has no dependency on scanner internals. E4 phase fields extend the common result model without requiring a different assertion API."
    dependencies[1]: E1.S4
    stories[3]:
      - id: E5.S1
        title: "Complete the small scalar, string, and collection assertion surface"
        details[6]: "Build on the fundamental assertions with assert_contains and assert_near, maintaining expected-first ordering wherever two comparable values are involved.","Define float behavior explicitly: require a nonnegative finite tolerance, fail comparisons involving NaN, and handle equal infinities deliberately. Avoid the POC diff > eps form that can accidentally pass NaN.",Use the language’s equality for comparable slices/arrays and format their values with existing Goose hooks. Do not add generic custom comparator registries.,Attach an optional user message to failures without losing generated expected/actual information. Keep comparison and recording separate from output rendering.,"Bound framework-controlled scalar and collection rendering and all retained diagnostic bytes. Treat custom format hooks as user code: they may do arbitrary work, so do not promise a memory bound inside the hook. Cap what is retained when it returns, and document that stage timeouts cover a nonterminating hook just as they cover a nonterminating test.","Use assert_contains(expected_fragment, actual_text, message = \"\") and assert_near(expected, actual, absolute_tolerance, message = \"\") as shown in the target API; do not copy actual-first ordering from the inline prototype."
        acceptanceCriteria[4]: "Common scalar, string, and collection failures show the expected and actual values with the correct labels.","Near comparisons cover zero tolerance, negative tolerance, NaN, equal/unequal infinities, and ordinary finite differences according to the documented contract.",A passing assertion does not format diagnostic values.,Oversized diagnostic values produce a visible limit outcome and cannot turn a failed assertion into a pass.
        unitTests[2]: Test comparison boundaries and float special values directly against structured failures.,Use a custom format hook with a counter to verify formatting only on failure.
        filesLikelyTouched[6]: src/runtime/assertions.goose,src/runtime/assertions_values.goose,src/runtime/diagnostics.goose,test/runtime/assertions_test.goose,test/runtime/diagnostics_test.goose,test/runtime/assertions_values_test.goose
        dependencies[1]: E1.S4
        risk: medium
        description: "**Design target:** [Concrete example in this plan](#reference-assertion-api).\n\n**Existing source references:**\n\n- [Expected-first assertion implementation — line 161, `fn assert_eq`](/Users/bob/repos/goosetest/poc/example_project/goosetest.goose)\n- [POC numeric comparison — line 214, `fn expect_near`](/Users/bob/repos/goosetest/poc/goosetest_poc.goose)\n- [POC string containment — line 225, `fn expect_contains`](/Users/bob/repos/goosetest/poc/goosetest_poc.goose)\n- [Style guide: const-generic parameters — line 576, `### 5.6`](/Users/bob/repos/goosefmt/docs/styleguide.md)\n\n**Retain or change:** Use the listed signature order consistently; correct the inline prototype’s NaN and argument-order pitfalls."
      - id: E5.S2
        title: Report mismatching struct fields through compile-time reflection
        details[5]: Implement assert_struct_eq using fields<T> and ordinary field equality. Report every differing top-level field in declaration order with expected and actual values.,"Describe the feature accurately as top-level field diffing. Do not promise recursive deep comparison, cyclic graph handling, or comparison of arbitrary uncomparable types.","For a nested comparable field, include its formatted value using the existing format hook; defer recursive path expansion.",Count the operation as one assertion and the containing case as one failure regardless of the number of differing fields.,Avoid repeated temporary strings and unnecessary copies. Use one bounded diagnostic builder and stop predictably if its capacity would be exceeded.
        acceptanceCriteria[4]: The POC permissions mismatch reports both .allowed and .reason in deterministic order.,Equal structs pass; a single mismatch and multiple mismatches produce the correct field list.,Read-only expected values and mutable actual values are accepted according to the verified compiler contract.,Unsupported field comparisons receive an honest compiler diagnostic; the framework does not invent unsafe bytewise equality.
        unitTests[2]: "Exercise fixed-size structs, slice fields, nested comparable structs, and a custom format hook.",Check that failure counts do not increase once per mismatching field.
        filesLikelyTouched[5]: src/runtime/assertions_struct.goose,src/runtime/diagnostics.goose,examples/basic/test/calculator_test.goose,test/runtime/diagnostics_test.goose,test/runtime/assertions_struct_test.goose
        dependencies[1]: E5.S1
        risk: medium
        description: "**Design target:** [Concrete example in this plan](#reference-permission-test).\n\n**Existing source references:**\n\n- [Top-level struct-field diff — line 209, `fn assert_struct_eq`](/Users/bob/repos/goosetest/poc/example_project/goosetest.goose)\n- [Permissions test and struct-diff example — line 30, `fn test_viewer_write_denied_demo_diff`](/Users/bob/repos/goosetest/poc/example_project/test/workspaces/permissions_test.goose)\n- [Style guide: compile-time reflection — line 837, `### 7.7`](/Users/bob/repos/goosefmt/docs/styleguide.md)\n\n**Retain or change:** Reproduce the two-field failure without promising recursive deep comparison."
      - id: E5.S3
        title: Render structured results with honest totals and locations
        details[5]: "Add a console renderer with deterministic suite/case ordering, pass/fail/skip labels, a final totals summary, and a separate section for failures and suite-hook errors.","Show original test path and declaration line, stable ID, display name where present, phase, user message, and expected/actual details. Do not imply that a declaration line is the exact failing assertion line.",Keep terminal control characters in user-provided labels and messages escaped so output stays legible. Use a portable plain-text baseline; no terminal UI dependency or required color support.,Render one case outcome after its cleanup completes. Keep compiler output distinct from framework summaries and ensure runner errors say when execution was incomplete.,"Do not add timing dashboards, telemetry, result databases, JSON output, TAP, JUnit XML, or reporter plugins. Structured internal records are sufficient to support a later exporter."
        acceptanceCriteria[5]: "A mixed suite reports reconciled passed, failed, skipped, and not-run counts without double-counting setup/cleanup errors.","The same result records render identically, independent of how they were collected.",Failure output points at the original test source and names setup/test/cleanup where relevant.,The renderer works with the E1 harness before CLI integration and with lifecycle results after E4.,"The six-case reference fixture reports 5 passed/1 skipped/status 0 normally, and 4 passed/1 failed/1 skipped/status 1 after the documented viewer-expectation mutation."
        unitTests[2]: Check deterministic output for representative structured records and escaped control characters.,Run a mixed lifecycle suite and compare the final displayed counts to the actual records.
