        dependencies[1]: E5.S2
        risk: medium
        description: "**Design target:** [Concrete example in this plan](#reference-console).\n\n**Existing source references:**\n\n- [POC console summary — line 235, `fn summary`](/Users/bob/repos/goosetest/poc/example_project/goosetest.goose)\n- [Permissions test and struct-diff example — line 30, `fn test_viewer_write_denied_demo_diff`](/Users/bob/repos/goosetest/poc/example_project/test/workspaces/permissions_test.goose)\n\n**Retain or change:** Match the information and actual exit status in the embedded positive/negative output scenarios."
    shortTitle: Assertions & reports
  - id: E6
    title: "Self-host, validate a real consumer, and document the release"
    summary: "Deliver a framework that tests itself, demonstrates adoption using goosefmt behavior, and has reproducible developer commands and documented limits."
    description: "<a id=\"reference-source-map\"></a>\n\n### Reference map: retain, change, and defer\n\nThese references identify the concrete source material for implementation review. Function/section names are the durable navigation keys; line numbers describe the files inspected when this plan was revised. Links open the original files, and the target snippets remain embedded above so the plan can be read on its own.\n\n| Reference | Retain | Change or defer | Stories |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| [Auth test authoring example — line 12, `struct Fixture`](/Users/bob/repos/goosetest/poc/example_project/test/auth/token_test.goose) | Test functions, fresh fixture, lifecycle names, readable labels. | Apply namespace/import/style rules and the explicit failure contract; defer tags/repetitions. | E1.S4, E4.S1–S4 |\n| [Permissions test and struct-diff example — line 30, `fn test_viewer_write_denied_demo_diff`](/Users/bob/repos/goosetest/poc/example_project/test/workspaces/permissions_test.goose) | Expected-first struct comparison and named mismatching fields. | Default example passes; intentional failure belongs in a negative fixture. | E5.S2–S3 |\n| [Runtime recovery boundary — line 56, `fn run_case`](/Users/bob/repos/goosetest/poc/example_project/goosetest.goose)<br>[Expected-first assertion implementation — line 161, `fn assert_eq`](/Users/bob/repos/goosetest/poc/example_project/goosetest.goose) | Static recovery boundary; hard versus soft assertions. | Namespaced context, narrow global bridges, bounded structured results, real process status. | E1.S1–S3 |\n| [POC directory walker — line 33, `fn walk_goose_files`](/Users/bob/repos/goosetest/poc/junit_runner_poc.goose)<br>[POC discovery and lifecycle emission — line 126, `fn scan_and_emit_suite`](/Users/bob/repos/goosetest/poc/junit_runner_poc.goose) | Automatic discovery and static generated calls. | Replace line-prefix parsing and silent caps with lexical scanning, diagnostics, and complete manifests. | E2.S1–S4, E3.S1 |\n| [POC mirror audit and generation — line 266, `fn generate_junit_runner`](/Users/bob/repos/goosetest/poc/junit_runner_poc.goose) | Project-relative unit correspondence and generated orchestration. | Separate integration area, stable authored root, owned generated files, actual compile/run stage. | E2.S3, E3.S1–S4 |\n| [Disabled call-graph preservation — line 224, `Keep disabled test in`](/Users/bob/repos/goosetest/poc/junit_runner_poc.goose) | Keep disabled tests under a valid assertion boundary for compilation. | Apply to filtered cases too; never execute their fixtures or hooks. | E4.S3–S4 |\n| [POC console summary — line 235, `fn summary`](/Users/bob/repos/goosetest/poc/example_project/goosetest.goose) | Grouped test outcomes, failure details, totals. | Separate rendering from runtime state; reconcile statuses and mark incomplete runs. | E5.S3, E6.S2 |\n| [Normative Goose style guide](/Users/bob/repos/goosefmt/docs/styleguide.md) | Normative modularity, imports, naming, data layout, bounded execution, formatting. | Do not inherit style violations or unsupported compiler assumptions from the POC. | All epics |\n| [Pinned compiler package/import contract — line 92, `**Only the root file declares packages.**`](/Users/bob/.local/share/goose/v2026.3/goose-v2026.3-macos-arm64/docs/packages.md)<br>[Pinned standard-library OS surface](/Users/bob/.local/share/goose/v2026.3/goose-v2026.3-macos-arm64/stdlib/os.goose) | Pinned import/sidecar semantics and verified standard-library operations. | Implement the missing CLI subprocess boundary rather than assume an API exists. | E1.S1, E3.S1–S2 |\n| [Existing goosefmt behavioral tests — line 89, `fn test_statement_blocks`](/Users/bob/repos/goosefmt/src/tests.goose)<br>[Existing goosefmt tokenizer — line 15, `struct Token`](/Users/bob/repos/goosefmt/src/lexer.goose) | Real grammar-sensitive formatter/lexer behavior and existing expected results. | Adapt in a disposable consumer fixture; do not mutate goosefmt. | E2.S2, E6.S3 |\n| [Inline describe/property/benchmark prototype](/Users/bob/repos/goosetest/poc/goosetest_poc.goose) | Useful comparison for assertion helpers and filters. | Nested DSL, properties, and benchmarks are outside this plan. | E5.S1 reference only |\n| [Deferred contained-worker alternative — line 46, `fn expect_abort`](/Users/bob/repos/goosetest/poc/goosetest_contained_poc.goose) | Evidence that contained abort handling is possible on a native build. | Expected-abort API and generalized crash isolation remain outside this release. | No implementation story in this release |\n\n**Implementation review:** compare the new public examples with the embedded references, compile them against the real package, exercise the documented positive and negative outcomes, and keep the example strings aligned with the actual API. This is behavioral verification, not a screenshot-only approval step.\n\nDelivery: a defensible first release on the explicitly supported platform. Adoption is demonstrated in an isolated fixture; editing another repository is not an implicit part of this implementation plan."
    dependencies[3]: E3.S4,E4.S4,E5.S3
    stories[4]:
      - id: E6.S1
        title: Move framework tests onto its own discovery and runner
        details[5]: Create a matching test/<path>/<name>_test.goose for every production .goose file under src/. Group each module’s edge cases and failure paths in its mirror.,Put cross-module and executable behavior under integration/. Keep supporting source/text fixtures under integration/fixtures/ or test_support/ and distinguish them from discovered test declarations.,Compile and run the framework’s goosetest_root.goose through the built CLI. Keep a small independent bootstrap verifier so the runtime cannot certify its own broken exit behavior.,Replace temporary manual registration used during development with generated discovery; retain a minimal explicit-harness example only where it tests the public runtime independently.,"Resolve the fixture-directory convention with E2: files containing intentionally invalid Goose should be stored as data with a non-.goose fixture suffix and materialized in temporary project directories."
        acceptanceCriteria[4]: The framework passes its own strict mirror audit and discovers all intended unit and integration suites.,All production modules have corresponding tests with actual valid test declarations.,"A deliberate assertion failure in a self-test causes a nonzero process result, verified outside goosetest.",Negative fixture data never appears as an accidentally discovered or linted test source.
        unitTests[2]: Run the complete self-hosted suite and compare listed IDs with the expected module/test inventory.,Temporarily inject a failing fixture in a disposable copy and verify external exit status.
